Randomness can be predicted by attackers, it can be used to manipulate the game, steal assets, or DDOS. The code below is a simplified version of the problematic code: 
                        function() payable {
                            uint256 random = now % 100;
                            msg.sender.transfer(random * msg.value);
                        }
                        In the above code, the attacker can predict the random value and use it to predict the amount of money to be sent. The attacker can send 1 ETH and get 99 ETH, or 2 ETH and get 98 ETH. The attacker can also send 99 ETH and get 1 ETH. 
                        A better approach would be to use a truly random number generation like this:
                        function() payable {
                            uint256 random = uint256(keccak256(block.timestamp, msg.sender)) % 100;
                            msg.sender.transfer(random * msg.value);
                        }
                        This way, the attacker can't predict the random value and always get their desired amount.
